            <ArticleTitle>Use of a bisphosphonate (etidronate) to improve metastatic bone pain in three hospice patients.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>333-5</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">This report investigates the association among the bisphosphonate etidronate and relief of metastatic bone pain. Maintaining cognitive function while providing adequate relief from metastatic bone pain can be particularly difficult in elderly patients.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">Case Series.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SETTING" NlmCategory="METHODS">Rural community dwellings (Carroll County, Maryland) and an urban nursing home (Baltimore, MD).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="PATIENTS" NlmCategory="METHODS">One patient residing in a skilled nursing facility and two patients from a home hospice program (Carroll Hospice).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="INTERVENTIONS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Administration of 400 mg of etidronate in cases of refractory metastatic bone pain.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MAIN OUTCOME MEASURES" NlmCategory="METHODS">Reduction in analgesic dosing, improvement in cognitive function, and subjective pain improvement.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Three cases are presented in which the use of the bisphosphonate etidronate allowed for a reduction in opiate use with improvement in pain control and preservation of cognitive function.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Etidronate and perhaps bisphosphonates, in general, should be considered in the management of refractory metastatic bone pain, particularly in the hospice population.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
